#: src/types-and-values/inference.md:6
msgid "Type Inference"
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/inference.md:8
msgid "Rust will look at how the variable is _used_ to determine the type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/inference.md:37
msgid ""
"This slide demonstrates how the Rust compiler infers types based on "
"constraints given by variable declarations and usages."
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/inference.md:40
msgid ""
"It is very important to emphasize that variables declared like this are not "
"of some sort of dynamic \"any type\" that can hold any data. The machine "
"code generated by such declaration is identical to the explicit declaration "
"of a type. The compiler does the job for us and helps us write more concise "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/inference.md:45
msgid ""
"When nothing constrains the type of an integer literal, Rust defaults to "
"`i32`. This sometimes appears as `{integer}` in error messages. Similarly, "
"floating-point literals default to `f64`."
msgstr ""

